WEBVTT

00:00:00.601 --> 00:00:01.470
Hello everyone.

00:00:01.708 --> 00:00:04.510
Today, we are talking about layered cuts.

00:00:04.839 --> 00:00:07.390
Clients with long hair very often ask:

00:00:07.739 --> 00:00:12.030
I want my hair lighter, with volume and movement, but don't cut it short.

00:00:12.377 --> 00:00:14.350
That is a very reasonable request.

00:00:14.698 --> 00:00:19.150
And to deliver it, the stylist must understand one thing really clearly:

00:00:19.488 --> 00:00:20.190
elevation.

00:00:20.806 --> 00:00:24.281
With a one-length cut, every strand stops at the same line.

00:00:24.705 --> 00:00:28.676
With a layered cut, the hair on top is shorter than the hair underneath,

00:00:28.626 --> 00:00:30.201
overlapping like roof tiles.

00:00:30.549 --> 00:00:33.641
The ends are lighter, and the hair has volume and movement.

00:00:34.084 --> 00:00:37.989
Long hair also feels less heavy, lies less flat against the head,

00:00:37.939 --> 00:00:40.361
and is easier to style when blow-drying.

00:00:40.701 --> 00:00:42.041
But layers remove weight.

00:00:42.147 --> 00:00:46.925
Too many layers on fine, thin hair leave the ends wispy and see-through,

00:00:46.875 --> 00:00:48.041
looking straggly.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:53.175
So before cutting, read the hair, as in the consultation and sectioning lesson.

00:00:53.663 --> 00:00:55.175
Thick, heavy hair:

00:00:55.587 --> 00:00:58.455
layers make it lighter, with less bulk at the ends.

00:00:58.864 --> 00:01:00.455
Fine, thin hair:

00:01:00.724 --> 00:01:04.215
fewer layers, and longer layers, to keep the ends thick.

00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:05.655
Curly hair:

00:01:06.004 --> 00:01:07.575
layers give the curls shape.

00:01:08.018 --> 00:01:11.612
But it shrinks a lot when dry, so cut it longer, or cut it dry,

00:01:11.562 --> 00:01:13.975
with a technique you have been trained in.

00:01:14.232 --> 00:01:16.215
And look at the whorl at the crown.

00:01:16.641 --> 00:01:19.895
Layers that are too short at a whorl stand straight up.

00:01:20.542 --> 00:01:21.902
Now, elevation:

00:01:22.243 --> 00:01:24.782
the angle at which you hold the hair away from the head.

00:01:25.186 --> 00:01:26.542
These are the common names.

00:01:26.811 --> 00:01:31.102
Every school measures and names them a little differently, but the idea is the same.

00:01:31.509 --> 00:01:32.622
0°:

00:01:32.811 --> 00:01:36.702
the hair falls straight down, giving a one-length line, like a bob.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.462
45°:

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:41.502
the hair stacks up in the lower part, building weight.

00:01:41.854 --> 00:01:44.622
This is called graduation, or low layers.

00:01:45.187 --> 00:01:46.222
90°:

00:01:46.564 --> 00:01:50.462
the hair is at a right angle to the scalp, exactly where you are cutting.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.462
The hair is the same length all over the head, following the head shape.

00:01:54.916 --> 00:01:56.862
This suits short and medium hair.

00:01:57.215 --> 00:02:00.222
On long hair, cutting like this means losing length.

00:02:00.728 --> 00:02:02.222
180°:

00:02:02.571 --> 00:02:05.022
the hair is lifted straight up to the top of the head.

00:02:05.208 --> 00:02:09.022
The hair on top is the shortest, getting longer the further down you go.

00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:12.462
These are long layers, and they keep the length of the hair.

00:02:13.127 --> 00:02:14.553
Next comes the guideline.

00:02:14.805 --> 00:02:15.913
There are two kinds.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.353
A traveling guideline:

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.833
each new section takes a little of the section you just cut, and is cut to match it.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:24.393
The guideline travels with you around the head.

00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:26.073
The result is even layers.

00:02:26.495 --> 00:02:27.833
A stationary guideline:

00:02:28.102 --> 00:02:29.969
the guideline stays in one place,

00:02:29.919 --> 00:02:33.273
and every section is pulled to exactly that place to be cut.

00:02:33.504 --> 00:02:36.873
The further the hair is from the guideline, the longer it gets.

00:02:37.124 --> 00:02:39.673
In practice, a haircut often uses both:

00:02:40.021 --> 00:02:43.113
a traveling guideline here, a stationary guideline there.

00:02:43.737 --> 00:02:45.120
That is over-direction.

00:02:45.363 --> 00:02:49.840
To keep length at the front, pull the front hair back, to a guideline at the back.

00:02:50.165 --> 00:02:51.440
The front hair will be longer.

00:02:51.845 --> 00:02:56.036
The other way round, if you want the hair around the face shorter, hugging the face,

00:02:55.986 --> 00:02:57.120
pull the hair forward.

00:02:57.469 --> 00:02:59.680
The hair at the back gets gradually longer.

00:02:59.935 --> 00:03:01.992
Many new stylists lose length at the front,

00:03:01.942 --> 00:03:05.120
just because they don't notice which way they are pulling the hair.

00:03:05.733 --> 00:03:07.201
The usual order is:

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:12.241
cut the outer perimeter first, at 0°, to exactly the agreed length.

00:03:12.497 --> 00:03:14.561
Only then cut the layers inside.

00:03:14.922 --> 00:03:17.441
The layers must connect softly to the perimeter.

00:03:17.789 --> 00:03:21.343
Layers cut too short compared with the perimeter leave the ends thin,

00:03:21.293 --> 00:03:23.121
with a break between the two parts.

00:03:23.748 --> 00:03:26.790
The face-framing layers are the part the client notices most,

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:29.045
because she sees them in the mirror every day.

00:03:29.454 --> 00:03:31.844
Agree with the client where the shortest layer starts:

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.285
at the cheekbone, at the chin, or at the collarbone.

00:03:35.545 --> 00:03:37.285
Start a little longer than you intend.

00:03:37.551 --> 00:03:38.565
You can always go shorter.

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:41.211
When cutting the hair at the front of the face,

00:03:41.161 --> 00:03:43.605
point the scissors away from the client's face,

00:03:43.874 --> 00:03:45.445
and ask her to close her eyes.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.226
You also have to think about how the client wears her hair at home.

00:03:49.649 --> 00:03:51.146
If she often ties it up:

00:03:51.381 --> 00:03:54.506
side layers that are too short will poke out of a ponytail.

00:03:54.933 --> 00:03:57.706
If she lets it dry naturally, without blow-drying:

00:03:57.975 --> 00:04:02.026
straight, fine hair with many short layers can easily look choppy.

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:05.226
Fewer, longer layers are easier to look after.

00:04:05.419 --> 00:04:07.230
Layer ends cut blunt look crisp,

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:10.586
while point cutting the ends of the layers makes them softer,

00:04:10.728 --> 00:04:13.226
so the layers blend into each other more naturally.

00:04:13.843 --> 00:04:15.960
Tension is the same as for a bob:

00:04:16.069 --> 00:04:17.320
light and even.

00:04:17.909 --> 00:04:21.400
Layers cut with strong tension shrink up once they are dry:

00:04:21.469 --> 00:04:23.080
shorter, and uneven.

00:04:23.589 --> 00:04:26.040
Keep the hair evenly damp with a spray bottle.

00:04:26.428 --> 00:04:28.752
Where some parts are dry and some are wet,

00:04:28.702 --> 00:04:31.080
the hair stretches and shrinks differently.

00:04:31.429 --> 00:04:32.920
And stand in the right place:

00:04:33.026 --> 00:04:35.320
your body right in front of the section you are cutting.

00:04:35.559 --> 00:04:38.832
Stand off to the side, and your hands pull off to the side,

00:04:38.782 --> 00:04:41.400
and the elevation changes without you noticing.

00:04:42.014 --> 00:04:42.926
Cross-checking.

00:04:43.366 --> 00:04:46.870
If you cut with vertical sections, check with horizontal sections,

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:48.126
and the other way round.

00:04:48.652 --> 00:04:50.766
Wherever something sticks out, trim it.

00:04:51.355 --> 00:04:53.406
Then check the balance on both sides:

00:04:53.754 --> 00:04:56.738
pull the two shortest pieces on each side of the face down,

00:04:56.688 --> 00:04:58.926
and see whether they are at the same height.

00:04:59.419 --> 00:05:01.326
After blow-drying, look again:

00:05:01.753 --> 00:05:05.086
is the volume even on both sides, is there a hole anywhere?

00:05:05.765 --> 00:05:07.425
Some very common mistakes.

00:05:07.854 --> 00:05:12.145
Layers on top that are too short, standing straight up, like wearing a hat.

00:05:12.696 --> 00:05:16.065
Ends that are wispy and thin, because of too much layering.

00:05:16.512 --> 00:05:18.705
The two sides of the face not balanced.

00:05:19.214 --> 00:05:22.385
And losing length at the front, from over-directing the wrong way.

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:25.810
All four mistakes can be avoided if you start long,

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:28.545
hold the hair at the right angle, and check often.

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:33.908
Scissors, combs and clips are cleaned and disinfected after every client,

00:05:33.858 --> 00:05:36.143
as in the lesson on cleaning tools.

00:05:36.572 --> 00:05:40.463
Every school and every country names and teaches this a little differently.

00:05:40.892 --> 00:05:42.463
This lesson is for practice;

00:05:42.652 --> 00:05:45.023
it does not replace a professional qualification.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:49.343
In the practice lesson, we will cut long layers, step by step.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:52.543
And now, please take the short quiz below the video.

00:05:52.811 --> 00:05:53.663
See you next time.
